{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; sed -n '1570,1625p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; sed -n '1570,1625p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # Run all tests (JVM, Node/CLJS, Dart/CLJD)\nbb test:clj          # JVM tests only (or: clj -M:test)\nbb test:cljs         # ClojureScript / Node tests (via shadow-cljs :cljs alias)\nbb test:cljd         # ClojureDart tests (requires flutter on PATH)\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n| `[t :assert tree-addr manifest]` — a module   | `[:db/add manifest :yin.module/exports tree-addr]`                                                                     |\n| manifest pins a tree                          |                                                                                                                        |\n+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n`:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name as a query alias for\n\"the `:yin.ledger/input` of a `:derive` event whose output is this\naddress\" and is no longer an attribute of the segment (`v2.cljc:327`,\n`linearize.cljc:212`).\n\n### 8.4 Expansion events keep their identity\n\n`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 records one event per attempt, successful or not,\nincluding repeated identical expansions, and links the event to its output\n(`yin.vm.macro.md:666-709`). This design keeps that contract: the\n`:expand` event entity **is** that event, one per attempt, and the address\nrows are attributes of it, not a replacement for it:\n\n```clojure\n[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op         :expand]\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input      tree-in-addr]        ; the tree containing the call\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-root  tree-in-addr]        ; the call occurrence, as values:\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-path  path]                ;   root address and structural path\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/origin     [:source medium batch j]]   ; INITIAL expansion only: a value\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent-event parent-ev]         ; NESTED expansion only: a declared ref, log-local\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent     parent-record-addr]  ; NESTED expansion only: the parent's record address, portable\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output     tree-out-addr]       ; on success\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/macro      lambda-addr]         ; the macro's content\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/attempt    [incarnation n]]     ; portable attempt identity, §8.4.1\n [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record     record-addr]         ; the addressed record of §8.2\n [:db/add ev :yin/source-batch      batch-id]            ; unchanged from §4.1\n [:db/add ev :yin/error             {...}]               ; on failure, unchanged\n [:db/add ev :yin/timestamp         ctx-t]]              ; unchanged\n```\n\nThe split between local references and portable values follows\n`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 exactly (`yin.vm.macro.md:692-708`), and it is\nenforced by how the transactor resolves ids: `apply-tempid-map` replaces a\ntempid in `v` **only when the whole value is a tempid on a declared\nreference attribute** (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:168-185`); a\ntempid nested inside a vector is left as it is. Therefore:\n\n- an **initial** expansion's call sits in admitted syntax, so it carries\n  `:yin.ledger/origin`, a value `[:source medium batch j]` including the\n  batch member index: the successor of `:yin/source-call` qualified by\n  `:yin/source-batch`, which no transactor resolves, as today;\n- a **nested** expansion's call sits in the output of the enclosing\n  expansion, so it carries **two** links to the parent: `:yin.ledger/\n  parent-event`, a declared reference attribute whose whole value is the\n  parent event's id, the successor of `:yin/source-node` and resolved by\n  the transactor within the log medium exactly as `:yin/source-node` is\n  today; and `:yin.ledger/parent`, the parent's **record address**, which\n  is a value and travels. The parent is complete before the child begins,\n  because a nested expansion's input is the enclosing expansion's output\n  (`yin.vm.macro.md:698-701`), so the parent's record address is known\n  when the child's record is minted; no staging after allocation is\n  needed, and the record (§8.2) never contains a log-local id, so it is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
